1
00:00:02,417 --> 00:00:03,238
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you want to talk about?

2
00:00:05,159 --> 00:00:06,120
[SPEAKER_00]: Shall we talk about impermanence?

3
00:00:06,981 --> 00:00:07,181
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

4
00:00:07,642 --> 00:00:08,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Or about anicca?

5
00:00:08,903 --> 00:00:12,546
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

6
00:00:18,051 --> 00:00:18,711
[SPEAKER_01]: What is anicca?

7
00:00:20,132 --> 00:00:20,933
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a good start.

8
00:00:22,034 --> 00:00:22,775
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh...

9
00:00:30,632 --> 00:00:36,674
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess my first translation, my first word, would be not permanent.

10
00:00:38,315 --> 00:00:45,698
[SPEAKER_00]: But then that needs lots of qualification, what that means.

11
00:00:47,539 --> 00:00:58,083
[SPEAKER_00]: So, something that's not permanent is of the nature to change.

12
00:00:59,232 --> 00:01:01,114
[SPEAKER_00]: Because something which is permanent doesn't change.

13
00:01:07,519 --> 00:01:27,615
[SPEAKER_00]: It's such an inconceivable of something being... Well, I think people can... yeah, people can think, okay, this is... well, they can assume, but if you had to stop and think about it, can you give example of something that's not subject to change?

14
00:01:27,955 --> 00:01:28,876
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no, but...

15
00:01:32,124 --> 00:01:40,732
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, so for instance, people might say, oh, impermanence means everything's changing.

16
00:01:41,813 --> 00:01:43,834
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't change.

17
00:01:43,854 --> 00:01:44,075
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

18
00:01:44,555 --> 00:01:44,895
[SPEAKER_00]: For them.

19
00:01:45,376 --> 00:01:45,676
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

20
00:01:46,797 --> 00:01:48,398
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why they're not seeing impermanence.

21
00:01:48,699 --> 00:01:48,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

22
00:01:53,583 --> 00:01:55,885
[SPEAKER_00]: But, is there a but?

23
00:01:57,555 --> 00:01:58,917
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is anicca?

24
00:01:58,937 --> 00:02:03,521
[SPEAKER_00]: Anicca would be, the way I see it, having the nature of change.

25
00:02:20,848 --> 00:02:22,689
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the Buddha says things like...

26
00:02:22,709 --> 00:02:36,595
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is matter, feeling, perception, the agnus, the I, the body, whatever... Is that nicca or anicca?

27
00:02:36,815 --> 00:02:40,656
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is it permanent?

28
00:02:41,357 --> 00:02:43,057
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, they don't change?

29
00:02:43,698 --> 00:02:45,318
[SPEAKER_00]: Or are they of the nature to change?

30
00:02:50,663 --> 00:03:00,611
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the problem with what I'm saying, we've already talked about this, is that then you have to be very careful about what we mean by change.

31
00:03:01,132 --> 00:03:01,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

32
00:03:02,433 --> 00:03:08,217
[SPEAKER_00]: But there's a problem before that as well, which is, it's not things are subject to change.

33
00:03:09,118 --> 00:03:10,619
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not the question the Buddha asks.

34
00:03:11,160 --> 00:03:11,340
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

35
00:03:12,361 --> 00:03:14,182
[SPEAKER_01]: He refers to the saṅkharas.

36
00:03:16,104 --> 00:03:17,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Are saṅkharas impermanent?

37
00:03:18,085 --> 00:03:19,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Are saṅkharas subjected to change?

38
00:03:20,737 --> 00:03:22,058
[SPEAKER_01]: That which determines...

39
00:03:22,078 --> 00:03:22,499
[SPEAKER_01]: The things.

40
00:03:22,959 --> 00:03:24,560
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

41
00:03:24,600 --> 00:03:26,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Things that you take it to be yours, things that you are...

42
00:03:26,942 --> 00:03:34,649
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in order then to arrive at the level where anicca applies, you need to reverse the order and you need to follow the right order.

43
00:03:36,370 --> 00:03:48,200
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if the Buddha's question, or statement, sabbe saṅkhara anicca, not sabbe dhamma anicca, sabbe saṅkhara anicca, sabbe saṅkhara dukkha, sabbe dhamma anatta,

44
00:03:49,439 --> 00:03:58,822
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if the starting point of puthujjana is sabbe dhamma atta, all things are me and myself, or for myself.

45
00:03:59,942 --> 00:04:05,164
[SPEAKER_01]: I might not go around saying I own everything, but I could own things if they come along, and I want to own them.

46
00:04:05,244 --> 00:04:16,628
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you imply the possibility of ownership, which is a possibility, which has implicit possibility of pleasure, satisfaction, everlasting permanence, or whatever else you want to translate it.

47
00:04:17,468 --> 00:04:19,909
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now, that's your starting attitude.

48
00:04:20,270 --> 00:04:22,511
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the starting attitude of somebody who wants to practice.

49
00:04:22,551 --> 00:04:30,475
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't now understand anicca on this, like, intellectual level, because you don't know where that level is correctly.

50
00:04:31,336 --> 00:04:44,303
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can certainly collect information about it, through reading suttas and so on, but in order to understand anicca, it only can be understood on the level of saṅkhara, not on the level of all the dhammas that you regard as yours.

51
00:04:45,900 --> 00:04:47,981
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you then arrive at the level of saṅkharas?

52
00:04:49,061 --> 00:04:52,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you just attend to it, oh, this is impermanent, that's another dhamma.

53
00:04:52,822 --> 00:04:57,284
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so... Saṅkharas are... how do we describe them before?

54
00:04:58,424 --> 00:04:59,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Did we describe them before at all?

55
00:05:02,963 --> 00:05:03,984
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought we spoke about it.

56
00:05:04,484 --> 00:05:04,845
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, we did.

57
00:05:04,865 --> 00:05:06,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it was about meditation.

58
00:05:06,146 --> 00:05:07,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

59
00:05:07,027 --> 00:05:08,748
[SPEAKER_01]: Your calming, your sankaras and whatnot.

60
00:05:08,768 --> 00:05:09,329
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

61
00:05:09,369 --> 00:05:10,149
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right, right.

62
00:05:10,169 --> 00:05:10,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

63
00:05:10,670 --> 00:05:11,110
[SPEAKER_00]: We did.

64
00:05:11,130 --> 00:05:14,593
[SPEAKER_00]: So, we're talking about that which determines your sense of self.

65
00:05:15,134 --> 00:05:15,314
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

66
00:05:15,494 --> 00:05:19,658
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the necessary basis for yourself, the way you know yourself.

67
00:05:20,443 --> 00:05:33,801
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for the necessary basis for your sense of self, necessary basis for your ownership, for your identity, necessary basis for your actions, for your choices, for your management techniques, necessary basis for practice as well, you've seen correctly.

68
00:05:35,113 --> 00:05:36,314
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what impermanence is.

69
00:05:36,754 --> 00:05:45,319
[SPEAKER_01]: So, unless you're seeing impermanence on that, like, indirectly, on the necessary basis for this, which I am attending to, you're not seeing impermanence.

70
00:05:45,799 --> 00:05:48,881
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just sort of pasting it over as a kind of attitude.

71
00:05:49,422 --> 00:05:51,043
[SPEAKER_01]: It will then cancel and deny everything.

72
00:05:51,703 --> 00:05:54,485
[SPEAKER_01]: And by doing so, also deny any problem that you might have.

73
00:05:54,765 --> 00:05:55,926
[SPEAKER_01]: So, everything is impermanent.

74
00:05:55,966 --> 00:05:57,086
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything will come to pass.

75
00:05:57,547 --> 00:05:59,728
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it doesn't matter that I suffer right now.

76
00:06:00,088 --> 00:06:00,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it matters.

77
00:06:00,709 --> 00:06:03,110
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you wouldn't be going around saying everything shall pass.

78
00:06:03,230 --> 00:06:05,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Why are you saying that, if nothing bothers you to begin with?

79
00:06:05,472 --> 00:06:06,893
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you care whether it passes or not?

80
00:06:07,393 --> 00:06:08,254
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you want it to go away.

81
00:06:08,274 --> 00:06:10,396
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you want it to go away, exactly, yeah.

82
00:06:10,836 --> 00:06:13,918
[SPEAKER_01]: It means you're not seeing impermanence, because you're looking at the wrong level.

83
00:06:13,958 --> 00:06:19,162
[SPEAKER_01]: You're looking at the level of things and dhammas and observable things you can attend and so on.

84
00:06:19,362 --> 00:06:21,183
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where you expect to see impermanence.

85
00:06:21,444 --> 00:06:23,005
[SPEAKER_01]: It can only be discerned peripherally.

86
00:06:23,505 --> 00:06:26,207
[SPEAKER_01]: On the level of the basis of things that make this possible.

87
00:06:26,587 --> 00:06:35,473
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I'm looking at things now, hearing things now, I like them, I dislike them, I want more of this, I don't want any of this.

88
00:06:35,893 --> 00:06:38,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is impermanence in regard to that experience?

89
00:06:40,036 --> 00:06:42,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Or where are the saṅkharas in regard to that experience?

90
00:06:45,119 --> 00:06:51,183
[SPEAKER_00]: I'd say, like, at the level, like, at the sort of experience as a whole.

91
00:06:52,300 --> 00:06:54,761
[SPEAKER_00]: Where is that?

92
00:06:55,082 --> 00:06:58,804
[SPEAKER_00]: In relation to what I'm looking, hearing, seeing, thinking?

93
00:06:58,824 --> 00:07:01,425
[SPEAKER_00]: The periphery.

94
00:07:01,645 --> 00:07:05,027
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they're indirect to my direction of attention.

95
00:07:05,187 --> 00:07:05,347
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

96
00:07:06,308 --> 00:07:08,469
[SPEAKER_01]: But, how would you then describe them?

97
00:07:10,270 --> 00:07:11,130
[SPEAKER_01]: Those indirect things.

98
00:07:11,150 --> 00:07:13,171
[SPEAKER_01]: What would be the unifying basis?

99
00:07:14,192 --> 00:07:14,552
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling.

100
00:07:15,292 --> 00:07:15,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling?

101
00:07:15,693 --> 00:07:16,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, yeah.

102
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:22,624
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the present enduring feeling that you were not creator of, it's already... it can already be a saṅkhara.

103
00:07:22,944 --> 00:07:23,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

104
00:07:23,804 --> 00:07:27,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Depending whether you are acting out of that feeling towards projected things and so on.

105
00:07:28,067 --> 00:07:30,509
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess if it's seen properly, then that's what feeling is, isn't it?

106
00:07:30,529 --> 00:07:30,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

107
00:07:30,669 --> 00:07:30,969
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

108
00:07:30,989 --> 00:07:31,409
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that way.

109
00:07:31,429 --> 00:07:35,211
[SPEAKER_01]: What's another thing that can also serve as a recognition of the level of saṅkhara?

110
00:07:35,231 --> 00:07:37,093
[SPEAKER_01]: The sense of self.

111
00:07:37,353 --> 00:07:38,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no.

112
00:07:38,093 --> 00:07:38,673
[SPEAKER_01]: Slightly more...

113
00:07:44,265 --> 00:07:53,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you see, hear, taste, touch, argue on account of it, agree on account of it, think about it, if your body is not there alive on its own beforehand?

114
00:07:56,492 --> 00:07:56,912
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's...

115
00:07:57,232 --> 00:07:58,032
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, we see, if I...

116
00:07:58,113 --> 00:07:59,673
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so my body is here.

117
00:08:00,113 --> 00:08:11,296
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, what you're looking... Again, what you're seeing is your sight, what you're hearing is basically your ears, which means that's not now that body because of which I can see, hear, taste, touch.

118
00:08:11,817 --> 00:08:15,838
[SPEAKER_01]: You failing to see that is you maintaining the attitude that I can see itself.

119
00:08:16,598 --> 00:08:17,819
[SPEAKER_01]: Ears can hear itself.

120
00:08:17,939 --> 00:08:21,242
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is inconceivable, even to Upatūjana, if he stops and thinks about it.

121
00:08:21,262 --> 00:08:21,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

122
00:08:21,962 --> 00:08:24,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the, like, the existentialist philosophers knew that.

123
00:08:24,885 --> 00:08:25,826
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, exactly.

124
00:08:25,906 --> 00:08:34,973
[SPEAKER_01]: So, saṅkhara, then, that is of the nature to change, is on that level, which you cannot directly attend to.

125
00:08:35,273 --> 00:08:36,934
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot observe impermanence.

126
00:08:36,994 --> 00:08:38,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Look, impermanent, impermanent.

127
00:08:38,656 --> 00:08:39,857
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that means it's out there.

128
00:08:39,877 --> 00:08:40,777
[SPEAKER_01]: It's already projected.

129
00:08:40,898 --> 00:08:43,580
[SPEAKER_01]: It, well, it allows you to not take it personally.

130
00:08:44,454 --> 00:08:49,760
[SPEAKER_01]: If you understand impermanence on the right level, you cannot not be affected by it on the emotional level.

131
00:08:50,341 --> 00:08:53,124
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why sābe, sankhāra, anicca, sābe, sankhāra, dukkha.

132
00:08:53,224 --> 00:08:56,889
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all of the determinations are unpleasant because of that.

133
00:08:57,469 --> 00:09:00,733
[SPEAKER_01]: Not sometimes like this, sometimes like that, sometimes they could be pleasant, sometimes... No.

134
00:09:01,174 --> 00:09:04,658
[SPEAKER_01]: Always, all of them are unpleasant because always, all of them

135
00:09:05,396 --> 00:09:08,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Remain subjected to that nature of abrupt change.

136
00:09:09,418 --> 00:09:13,080
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, you have been... they've been thrust upon you.

137
00:09:13,100 --> 00:09:16,502
[SPEAKER_01]: They've been... you've been subjected to... You've been... yeah, you're always secondary to them.

138
00:09:16,522 --> 00:09:17,943
[SPEAKER_01]: You're always contained within them.

139
00:09:18,503 --> 00:09:24,406
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you look for impermanence in things you can see, hear, taste, smell, taste, touch, and so on, you're not going to find it.

140
00:09:24,867 --> 00:09:27,408
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly how people then fall into a view of

141
00:09:27,808 --> 00:09:30,890
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, look, the modern science says that everything is constantly changing.

142
00:09:31,211 --> 00:09:32,411
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the Buddha meant.

143
00:09:33,052 --> 00:09:34,893
[SPEAKER_01]: So, does the science make you sotāpanna?

144
00:09:34,913 --> 00:09:37,035
[SPEAKER_01]: Does the science free you from sensuality and suffering?

145
00:09:37,335 --> 00:09:37,555
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

146
00:09:37,775 --> 00:09:38,556
[SPEAKER_00]: Not even close.

147
00:09:39,617 --> 00:09:43,199
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, here you are, holding a view that you understood in permanence that the Buddha was teaching.

148
00:09:43,760 --> 00:09:48,083
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and why does the Buddha talk so much about mindfulness of the feeling, mindfulness of the body?

149
00:09:48,243 --> 00:09:49,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

150
00:09:49,364 --> 00:09:51,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the way to discern anicca.

151
00:09:51,245 --> 00:09:53,547
[SPEAKER_00]: Peripherally, where you can discern the anicca.

152
00:09:53,727 --> 00:09:55,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you attend to it directly,

153
00:09:56,253 --> 00:09:58,396
[SPEAKER_01]: Something else determines what you're attending at.

154
00:09:58,416 --> 00:09:59,637
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how attention works.

155
00:10:00,478 --> 00:10:03,621
[SPEAKER_01]: In order for you to attend something, it needs to be given as that possibility.

156
00:10:04,041 --> 00:10:08,906
[SPEAKER_01]: And anicca is on the level of the possibilities, the level of the nature, subjected to, and so on.

157
00:10:09,627 --> 00:10:10,448
[SPEAKER_00]: So, would you say thus?

158
00:10:11,704 --> 00:10:13,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Yoniso manasikāra, one of the conditions.

159
00:10:13,245 --> 00:10:14,727
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, of course, absolutely.

160
00:10:15,227 --> 00:10:18,930
[SPEAKER_01]: Yoniso manasikāra is recognition of the peripheral in regard to my direct attention.

161
00:10:19,330 --> 00:10:26,496
[SPEAKER_01]: What I'm looking at, and now I'm discerning other things without needing to move and look at them directly, because then that would be actual and something else is peripheral.

162
00:10:26,556 --> 00:10:26,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

163
00:10:27,898 --> 00:10:34,043
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's tend... attending to the kind of origin from which these things

164
00:10:36,062 --> 00:10:36,783
[SPEAKER_00]: What I'm attending to...

165
00:10:36,803 --> 00:10:37,243
[SPEAKER_00]: Discerning.

166
00:10:37,404 --> 00:10:38,485
[SPEAKER_00]: Discerning the origin.

167
00:10:38,885 --> 00:10:39,425
[SPEAKER_00]: What did I say?

168
00:10:39,566 --> 00:10:39,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Attending.

169
00:10:40,166 --> 00:10:40,306
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

170
00:10:40,386 --> 00:10:40,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

171
00:10:40,647 --> 00:10:41,087
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry, okay.

172
00:10:41,107 --> 00:10:41,427
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's...

173
00:10:41,467 --> 00:10:43,509
[SPEAKER_00]: There will be attention attending to itself.

174
00:10:43,569 --> 00:10:44,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

175
00:10:44,630 --> 00:10:45,291
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

176
00:10:45,371 --> 00:10:57,061
[SPEAKER_00]: I, seeing itself, sort of, recognize discerning what... what... what... that the, sort of, origin from which, whatever it is I'm attending to, is what it is, to be understood in that way.

177
00:10:57,081 --> 00:11:00,645
[SPEAKER_00]: An origin meaning the necessary basis that still has to be there.

178
00:11:00,685 --> 00:11:00,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

179
00:11:01,942 --> 00:11:11,152
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, would you be able to continue seeing getting elated on account of your sights, depressed on account of your sights, indifferent on account of your sights, if your eyes are still not there and you're in there?

180
00:11:11,973 --> 00:11:13,394
[SPEAKER_01]: Would that be even conceivable?

181
00:11:13,474 --> 00:11:13,995
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely not.

182
00:11:15,576 --> 00:11:23,605
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, while I'm seeing, while I'm looking at these things, I have the knowledge that all of this, actually,

183
00:11:24,443 --> 00:11:26,184
[SPEAKER_01]: It's present, because my eyes are still there.

184
00:11:26,505 --> 00:11:33,130
[SPEAKER_01]: And all the possibilities that I were to engage on, in, on account of my sights, would be because these eyes are still there.

185
00:11:34,211 --> 00:11:43,718
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what impermanence is, on the level of that knowledge of these eyes are still there as a necessary basis for any movement towards them, against them, both, neither, whichever way.

186
00:11:43,838 --> 00:11:44,979
[SPEAKER_01]: I want, I don't want.

187
00:11:45,519 --> 00:11:47,421
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because the eyes are still there.

188
00:11:47,601 --> 00:11:47,781
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

189
00:11:48,342 --> 00:11:48,662
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

190
00:11:49,022 --> 00:11:49,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you conceive?

191
00:11:50,383 --> 00:11:51,404
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you even imagine?

192
00:11:53,471 --> 00:12:01,516
[SPEAKER_01]: To be in control of the eye organ, in a sense of to prevent it from being subjected to accidents, for example.

193
00:12:01,536 --> 00:12:05,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can protect, but only with the extent of hope that it will work.

194
00:12:06,339 --> 00:12:10,381
