00:24:07,435 --> 00:24:08,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would it feel awful?

390
00:24:08,636 --> 00:24:10,116
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not directly threatening you, isn't it?

391
00:24:10,136 --> 00:24:10,516
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's true.

392
00:24:10,536 --> 00:24:13,137
[SPEAKER_01]: You're still alive, because... You can still breathe.

393
00:24:13,257 --> 00:24:13,777
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

394
00:24:14,538 --> 00:24:15,278
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the issue then?

395
00:24:17,419 --> 00:24:27,622
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're still wanting that control, which feels like it's been taken away from you.

396
00:24:27,642 --> 00:24:29,463
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're basically still assuming control.

397
00:24:30,923 --> 00:24:32,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

398
00:24:32,024 --> 00:24:35,345
[SPEAKER_01]: The fear, the dread you would feel in such a situation would be the...

399
00:24:36,373 --> 00:24:40,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Directly corresponding to the discrepancy you created with your own assumption of it.

400
00:24:40,934 --> 00:24:48,115
[SPEAKER_01]: The assumption being external to this coffin.

401
00:24:48,575 --> 00:24:51,616
[SPEAKER_01]: So, imagine now, when actually that is the reality.

402
00:24:52,956 --> 00:24:57,237
[SPEAKER_01]: You are, as the Buddha said, the four mountains are squeezing us.

403
00:24:58,137 --> 00:25:02,138
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's inconceivable that you can, you know, go over them, or avoid them, or evade them.

404
00:25:02,478 --> 00:25:03,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable.

405
00:25:03,519 --> 00:25:05,059
[SPEAKER_01]: You're gonna get crushed.

406
00:25:05,079 --> 00:25:05,879
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, literally.

407
00:25:06,722 --> 00:25:11,225
[SPEAKER_01]: You will always remain inside of that, to the point where you won't be, because of it.

408
00:25:11,565 --> 00:25:12,546
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, that first.

409
00:25:12,986 --> 00:25:13,506
[SPEAKER_00]: That's first.

410
00:25:13,526 --> 00:25:18,989
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are, actually, your existence, anyone's existence, it's closer to being completely in a coffin.

411
00:25:19,090 --> 00:25:25,674
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you cannot conceive the outside of this body, the outside of these senses, the outside of these thoughts, so to speak.

412
00:25:27,295 --> 00:25:33,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, here I am, maintaining a notion that I am master controller in regard to them.

413
00:25:33,058 --> 00:25:33,678
[SPEAKER_01]: They obey me.

414
00:25:33,738 --> 00:25:34,259
[SPEAKER_01]: I want this.

415
00:25:34,339 --> 00:25:34,999
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want that.

416
00:25:35,059 --> 00:25:35,580
[SPEAKER_01]: I choose this.

417
00:25:35,660 --> 00:25:36,160
[SPEAKER_01]: I choose that.

418
00:25:38,160 --> 00:25:40,001
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that is the starting point.

419
00:25:41,322 --> 00:25:42,483
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you realize then...

420
00:25:42,503 --> 00:25:44,144
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not about denying your control.

421
00:25:44,244 --> 00:25:45,584
[SPEAKER_01]: I have no control over myself.

422
00:25:45,965 --> 00:25:46,145
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

423
00:25:46,605 --> 00:25:47,866
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just putting it in perspective.

424
00:25:48,266 --> 00:26:06,037
[SPEAKER_01]: Perspective being that no amount of your control, no amount of your possessions, no amount of your ownership, no amount of your satisfaction through sensuality can ever put you outside of the senses, outside of the given body, outside of the coffin, basically, that you're buried in.

425
00:26:08,498 --> 00:26:18,081
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you think the Buddha said it's like the existence is like constantly being... like a cow being... a flayed cow being eaten by insects from any direction.

426
00:26:18,201 --> 00:26:20,582
[SPEAKER_01]: There's absolutely no... nowhere that it can rest.

427
00:26:21,042 --> 00:26:22,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether it sits, stands...

428
00:26:22,142 --> 00:26:22,923
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's it.

429
00:26:22,963 --> 00:26:23,803
[SPEAKER_01]: You can still do all this.

430
00:26:23,843 --> 00:26:24,543
[SPEAKER_01]: You can move around.

431
00:26:24,583 --> 00:26:25,904
[SPEAKER_01]: You can turn around in your coffin.

432
00:26:26,084 --> 00:26:26,324
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

433
00:26:27,464 --> 00:26:28,004
[SPEAKER_01]: You can breathe.

434
00:26:28,024 --> 00:26:28,504
[SPEAKER_01]: You can think.

435
00:26:28,524 --> 00:26:29,085
[SPEAKER_01]: You can sing.

436
00:26:29,125 --> 00:26:30,985
[SPEAKER_01]: You can do what you like in your coffin.

437
00:26:31,265 --> 00:26:31,545
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

438
00:26:33,126 --> 00:26:36,587
[SPEAKER_01]: And often, actually, people's existence is not that different.

439
00:26:37,408 --> 00:26:43,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Things you do is just scratch an immediate itch, eat when I'm bored, sleep when I want to sleep, distract myself.

440
00:26:43,373 --> 00:26:44,354
[SPEAKER_01]: All that you can do in a coffin.

441
00:26:45,535 --> 00:26:47,136
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is one frightening, one isn't?

442
00:26:48,417 --> 00:26:51,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is one person frightened and another not?

443
00:26:51,239 --> 00:26:53,801
[SPEAKER_01]: No, why is one recognition frightening, the other one isn't?

444
00:26:55,503 --> 00:26:59,686
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you recognize, if you take that you are enclosed first, that is frightening.

445
00:27:01,007 --> 00:27:02,969
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you don't take that first, that's not frightening.

446
00:27:03,069 --> 00:27:04,490
[SPEAKER_00]: Although you go about doing the same thing.

447
00:27:04,510 --> 00:27:05,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I see, I see.

448
00:27:06,865 --> 00:27:07,586
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're ignoring.

449
00:27:08,907 --> 00:27:09,908
[SPEAKER_00]: No, but why is it frightening?

450
00:27:10,688 --> 00:27:15,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it frightening to take that being in a coffin first?

451
00:27:16,573 --> 00:27:21,397
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that undermines your assumption that you are in control.

452
00:27:22,258 --> 00:27:24,099
[SPEAKER_00]: And why is that assumption then there?

453
00:27:24,800 --> 00:27:25,741
[SPEAKER_00]: That you are in control?

454
00:27:26,561 --> 00:27:27,342
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's gratuitous.

455
00:27:27,883 --> 00:27:28,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

456
00:27:29,684 --> 00:27:31,806
[SPEAKER_00]: Beginningless, but completely

457
00:27:32,999 --> 00:27:33,459
[SPEAKER_00]: Completely.

458
00:27:33,539 --> 00:27:34,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Utterly.

459
00:27:34,180 --> 00:27:35,600
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I guess, why is that?

460
00:27:35,620 --> 00:27:36,441
[SPEAKER_00]: To avoid the pain?

461
00:27:36,981 --> 00:27:37,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

462
00:27:37,701 --> 00:27:38,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, sure.

463
00:27:38,342 --> 00:27:39,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Of... of...

464
00:27:39,562 --> 00:27:45,125
[SPEAKER_01]: It has its reasons, but the thing is, the only reason it can be there, is... is because you keep maintaining it.

465
00:27:45,445 --> 00:27:45,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

466
00:27:45,765 --> 00:27:47,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Through ignoring the fact that you are in the coffin first.

467
00:27:47,826 --> 00:27:48,306
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

468
00:27:49,067 --> 00:27:49,467
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

469
00:27:49,567 --> 00:27:49,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

470
00:27:53,244 --> 00:28:06,282
[SPEAKER_01]: And once you... once you understand that this is the necessary order... well, also, like, puts in perspective when people... remember in the suttas, when they hear the Buddha, and they get a glimpse of what he's actually saying, and they freak out.

471
00:28:06,863 --> 00:28:07,684
[SPEAKER_01]: They fall on the ground.

472
00:28:08,145 --> 00:28:08,365
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

473
00:28:08,465 --> 00:28:09,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Overwhelmed by anxiety.

474
00:28:10,311 --> 00:28:13,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Start screaming, well, they will kill me, they will crush me, they will destroy me.

475
00:28:13,414 --> 00:28:15,035
[SPEAKER_01]: I am buried alive while I'm alive.

476
00:28:15,896 --> 00:28:18,298
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, my life is my burial, basically.

477
00:28:18,778 --> 00:28:18,979
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

478
00:28:19,459 --> 00:28:20,480
[SPEAKER_00]: I cannot...

479
00:28:20,500 --> 00:28:21,040
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm subjected to it.

480
00:28:21,060 --> 00:28:22,922
[SPEAKER_00]: My greatest fear is here right now.

481
00:28:23,182 --> 00:28:23,442
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

482
00:28:23,803 --> 00:28:24,003
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

483
00:28:25,084 --> 00:28:28,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, they will kill us, they will destroy us, they will make us... Yeah, exactly.

484
00:28:28,327 --> 00:28:28,747
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

485
00:28:29,007 --> 00:28:35,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you gratuitously assume the external of this which... within which you're fully enclosed, of your senses, of your body.

486
00:28:36,157 --> 00:28:37,718
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you know external of your body?

487
00:28:37,778 --> 00:28:40,819
[SPEAKER_01]: Right now, while we speak, your body is your coffin, basically.

488
00:28:40,919 --> 00:28:42,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

489
00:28:42,040 --> 00:28:43,500
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, that's what Buddha said in different words.

490
00:28:44,121 --> 00:28:46,302
[SPEAKER_01]: The entire universe is within this body.

491
00:28:46,342 --> 00:28:48,422
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you conceive outside of my body?

492
00:28:48,703 --> 00:28:50,303
[SPEAKER_00]: How is that even imaginable?

493
00:28:50,783 --> 00:28:54,265
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you can imagine it, because that imagination requires you to ignore the truth.

494
00:28:55,125 --> 00:28:55,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

495
00:28:55,726 --> 00:28:58,187
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's a thought which is...

496
00:28:59,147 --> 00:29:01,988
[SPEAKER_01]: But even that thought, yeah, cannot really take you outside.

497
00:29:02,048 --> 00:29:06,389
[SPEAKER_01]: It can just... Again, whether you realize or not, you are in the coffin.

498
00:29:06,609 --> 00:29:06,809
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

499
00:29:07,049 --> 00:29:07,569
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the thing.

500
00:29:08,189 --> 00:29:14,250
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only reason you experience fear on account of it, is because you still refuse to give up these ridiculous, gratuitous views.

501
00:29:14,650 --> 00:29:15,591
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you suffer.

502
00:29:15,631 --> 00:29:16,571
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because you're in the coffin.

503
00:29:16,591 --> 00:29:17,731
[SPEAKER_01]: You've always been in the coffin.

504
00:29:18,011 --> 00:29:18,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Always.

505
00:29:18,991 --> 00:29:24,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Fully enclosed within this body, and you are subjected to whatever that body gets subjected to.

506
00:29:25,793 --> 00:29:27,233
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inconceivable for you to

507
00:29:29,172 --> 00:29:40,341
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, accept that you are, whether you like it or not, stuck in a coffin.

508
00:29:40,901 --> 00:29:41,081
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

509
00:29:41,181 --> 00:29:44,784
[SPEAKER_01]: That you are secondary to the enclosure that your body provides.

510
00:29:45,105 --> 00:29:47,707
[SPEAKER_01]: And whenever you think outside of this body,

511
00:29:48,531 --> 00:29:49,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, oh, this is wrong.

512
00:29:49,632 --> 00:29:49,832
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

513
00:29:49,912 --> 00:29:56,836
[SPEAKER_01]: You can think, but make sure that that thought does not imply as an actual outside of the body, because such thing is inconceivable.

514
00:29:57,437 --> 00:30:00,198
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you will stop misconceiving in that.

515
00:30:00,238 --> 00:30:01,419
[SPEAKER_01]: You will stop conceiving it.

516
00:30:02,260 --> 00:30:03,000
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Buddha meant.

517
00:30:03,400 --> 00:30:04,441
[SPEAKER_01]: It's by misconceiving.

518
00:30:05,662 --> 00:30:19,386
[SPEAKER_00]: So, mindfulness of the body, recognizing the body as a saṅkhara for whatever it is I'm thinking, including this thought of outside this body, that's how you, again, stop going.

519
00:30:19,606 --> 00:30:24,308
[SPEAKER_01]: Any thought about outside of the body is within the experience of this body being here.

520
00:30:25,568 --> 00:30:27,749
[SPEAKER_01]: When I think outside, that's already internal.

521
00:30:29,372 --> 00:30:33,096
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly for somebody who's fully developed that, purified from all ignoring.

522
00:30:34,037 --> 00:30:44,750
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha said, for another hand, what is left is name and form, externally, that he can observe, and this conscious body.

523
00:30:45,240 --> 00:30:45,821
[SPEAKER_01]: The container.

524
00:30:46,001 --> 00:30:46,161
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

525
00:30:46,321 --> 00:30:47,682
[SPEAKER_01]: This conscious body, the container.

526
00:30:48,123 --> 00:30:56,811
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, within that container, yeah, there are things that are external-external, and there are things that are slightly less external, as in things that pertain to this body, and things that pertain to other bodies.

527
00:30:56,871 --> 00:30:59,353
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's all within this this conscious body as a basis.

528
00:30:59,473 --> 00:30:59,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

529
00:31:01,635 --> 00:31:05,459
[SPEAKER_01]: And when this conscious body breaks apart, for an arhant, that's nibbāna.

530
00:31:06,240 --> 00:31:10,925
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no more reappearing, no more being... Well, again, it's not that you get re-contained.

531
00:31:11,746 --> 00:31:14,189
[SPEAKER_01]: You always remain in the same container with the five aggregates.

532
00:31:14,589 --> 00:31:16,051
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the nature of rebirth.

533
00:31:17,032 --> 00:31:23,398
[SPEAKER_01]: Just remaining in the same container with different functional arrangements within the container, but it's still the same container.

534
00:31:24,059 --> 00:31:28,682
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you remember one lifetime, five hundred lifetimes, the Buddha said, all you're remembering is these five aggregates.

535
00:31:28,722 --> 00:31:30,543
[SPEAKER_01]: These five aggregates that you can see now.

536
00:31:30,783 --> 00:31:38,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, it's like this... No outside to it, doesn't matter how many eons have spent, how many rebirths you've been through, no out... inconceivable.

537
00:31:38,148 --> 00:31:44,331
[SPEAKER_01]: You, if you think outside, that thought conceives outside in regard to inside.

538
00:31:44,371 --> 00:31:44,792
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

539
00:31:44,852 --> 00:31:49,514
[SPEAKER_00]: And everything, so everything there, everything inside, is of the nature of abrupt change.

540
00:31:50,915 --> 00:31:51,796
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, because why?

541
00:31:53,693 --> 00:32:01,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the container that you cannot get under, repossess, step outside, avoid, is fully subject to change.

542
00:32:01,546 --> 00:32:08,497
[SPEAKER_01]: The saṅkhara is the container that determines your life, the possibilities in your life, the choices you make, you made, you're about to make.

543
00:32:09,289 --> 00:32:12,311
[SPEAKER_01]: All of it, that container, is not in your control.

544
00:32:13,032 --> 00:32:14,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Utterly, utterly inconceivable.

545
00:32:14,833 --> 00:32:20,516
[SPEAKER_01]: And you, thinking outside, can only be done on the basis of being enclosed within this, and then you project, oh, there must be outside.

546
00:32:20,556 --> 00:32:23,238
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can never actually step outside.

547
00:32:23,858 --> 00:32:27,721
[SPEAKER_01]: And the sooner you stop expecting that, the sooner you start practicing the dhamma correctly.

548
00:32:33,599 --> 00:32:37,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the way out is not digging the tunnel out, because there is no out.

549
00:32:37,782 --> 00:32:39,664
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you dig a tunnel out of your own body?

550
00:32:39,684 --> 00:32:39,904
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

551
00:32:40,024 --> 00:32:40,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable.

552
00:32:41,105 --> 00:32:43,327
[SPEAKER_01]: The way out is not being bothered.

553
00:32:43,347 --> 00:32:43,527
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

554
00:32:43,647 --> 00:32:48,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Emotionally, not resisting the discomfort of that which is subjected to change.

555
00:32:48,871 --> 00:32:50,473
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the way out is to understand dukkha.

556
00:32:51,273 --> 00:32:51,433
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

557
00:32:52,014 --> 00:32:53,515
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, how many times we all read that?

558
00:32:54,276 --> 00:32:57,939
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you say it's the way out is to stop wanting to get out?

559
00:33:01,314 --> 00:33:01,554
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

560
00:33:01,974 --> 00:33:05,295
[SPEAKER_01]: If wanting to get out means craving against what's currently felt.

561
00:33:05,315 --> 00:33:05,555
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

562
00:33:06,076 --> 00:33:06,636
[SPEAKER_01]: Unpleasantly.

563
00:33:10,117 --> 00:33:11,938
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's good that you want to get out.

564
00:33:12,938 --> 00:33:15,459
[SPEAKER_01]: What is not good is the way you choose to go about it.

565
00:33:15,859 --> 00:33:17,840
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, there is an ignorant way and there's a non-ignorant way.

566
00:33:17,860 --> 00:33:18,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

567
00:33:18,320 --> 00:33:20,020
[SPEAKER_01]: But wanting to get out is what the Buddha meant.

568
00:33:20,060 --> 00:33:22,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving is to be abandoned by the means of craving.

569
00:33:22,601 --> 00:33:23,601
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not about, like...

570
00:33:23,621 --> 00:33:27,263
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not about getting out, but it's just about not... Not being bothered and being closed.

571
00:33:27,283 --> 00:33:28,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Not being bothered in the slightest.

572
00:33:28,343 --> 00:33:28,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

573
00:33:29,503 --> 00:33:30,204
[SPEAKER_01]: And how do you arrive

574
00:33:32,013 --> 00:33:36,057
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you also develop that utter not being bothered by whether you live or die?

575
00:33:37,519 --> 00:33:38,780
[SPEAKER_01]: The utter equanimity.

576
00:33:39,922 --> 00:33:40,703
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness of death.

577
00:33:43,045 --> 00:33:44,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you think he was so potent?

578
00:33:45,087 --> 00:33:48,571
[SPEAKER_01]: Did Buddha said, like, you know, very quickly you result in complete dispassion?

579
00:33:49,172 --> 00:33:51,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Or a bit of madness as well, depending on whether you were ready or not.

580
00:33:53,772 --> 00:33:55,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that is the nature of things.

581
00:33:56,074 --> 00:33:57,295
[SPEAKER_01]: You are fully enclosed.

582
00:33:58,175 --> 00:33:58,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Within death.

583
00:33:59,056 --> 00:33:59,536
[SPEAKER_01]: Within death.

584
00:33:59,636 --> 00:34:02,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Within that, you're subjected to disappear, which is what death is.

585
00:34:02,699 --> 00:34:02,879
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

586
00:34:07,102 --> 00:34:12,146
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, everything you do in your life, unless it's dealing with that, is distracting you from it.

587
00:34:12,286 --> 00:34:12,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

588
00:34:14,127 --> 00:34:14,908
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just automatic.

589
00:34:15,068 --> 00:34:17,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you don't want to be distracted, if you're doing something

590
00:34:18,338 --> 00:34:26,524
[SPEAKER_01]: On the basis of ignoring these natures of these things, necessary for my action to begin with, that's how you maintain your own ignorance.

591
00:34:26,544 --> 00:34:26,724
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

592
00:34:26,944 --> 00:34:27,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you don't want to.

593
00:34:32,048 --> 00:34:42,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, again, can you be, I don't know, building a family, pursuing a job, career, being a monk, any of it, if you're not alive?

594
00:34:44,097 --> 00:34:45,918
[SPEAKER_01]: If your life is to be taken away from you.

595
00:34:47,630 --> 00:34:48,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you have any say in that?

596
00:34:49,891 --> 00:34:51,171
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you have any say in that?

597
00:34:51,191 --> 00:34:53,412
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only relatively protect it.

598
00:34:54,132 --> 00:35:01,695
[SPEAKER_01]: But even that, you realize, like, even the threats that you're subjected to, you can't even fathom that.

599
00:35:02,396 --> 00:35:07,598
[SPEAKER_01]: So you only have these projected ideas of threats, which means a projected idea of defending these threats.

600
00:35:08,218 --> 00:35:13,940
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's already so far removed from what you're actually constantly, even while you're defending yourself, subjected to.

601
00:35:17,003 --> 00:35:18,464
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you tell your heart how to beat?

602
00:35:20,586 --> 00:35:22,607
[SPEAKER_01]: When to stop, to beat faster?

603
00:35:22,627 --> 00:35:26,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, can you remain alive if that muscle in your chest stops beating?

604
00:35:28,431 --> 00:35:36,336
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all you need to do is not forget that whenever you're about to do something, whenever you're about to make a choice.

605
00:35:36,916 --> 00:35:42,580
[SPEAKER_01]: What's that going to result in is you will not be able to act out of passion, aversion and desire for distraction.

606
00:35:42,600 --> 00:35:45,762
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, again, distraction, act of distraction,

607
00:35:46,277 --> 00:35:48,778
[SPEAKER_01]: is within the container now, that you're not ignoring.

608
00:35:49,858 --> 00:35:54,719
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you distract yourself, if you were not there alive to begin with?

609
00:35:55,359 --> 00:35:55,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable.

610
00:35:56,640 --> 00:36:00,280
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even your means of escape, relative escape, are fully enclosed.

611
00:36:00,300 --> 00:36:02,141
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not escaping, you cannot escape anything.

612
00:36:02,601 --> 00:36:10,423
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why those people freaked out, when they understood, oh, inescapable, their will, their will for sure, guaranteed, kill me, destroy me.

613
00:36:12,201 --> 00:36:13,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I cannot conceive outside of this.

614
00:36:13,822 --> 00:36:15,904
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, I've been going around as if I am outside.

615
00:36:20,167 --> 00:36:21,167
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what anicca is.

616
00:36:23,109 --> 00:36:30,414
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the reason why we kind of don't want to use permanent-impermanent is because permanent-impermanent is on the level of people's perceptions.

617
00:36:31,135 --> 00:36:32,255
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, look, that's impermanent.

618
00:36:32,276 --> 00:36:33,116
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... No.

619
00:36:33,717 --> 00:36:35,378
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on the level of the nature of things.

620
00:36:36,267 --> 00:36:39,169
[SPEAKER_01]: Once people understand that, then, yeah, you can translate it whichever way you want.

621
00:36:40,269 --> 00:36:48,074
[SPEAKER_01]: But it must have that personal connotation, that undermining your sense of ownership.

622
00:36:48,354 --> 00:36:48,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

623
00:36:54,537 --> 00:36:58,700
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why then, sabhe saṅkhara nitya, sabhe saṅkhara dhūt, sabhe dhamma anātā.

624
00:37:00,247 --> 00:37:05,555
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all things that I can engage with, take, leave, want, not want.

625
00:37:05,855 --> 00:37:14,088
[SPEAKER_01]: All things cannot be mine, cannot be my own self, because they're undermined by the container that I cannot even conceive controlling.

626
00:37:21,588 --> 00:37:31,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's another thing you might wonder, like, okay, so you're not mindful of the body that's subjected to these things, it's kind of... the extent of it is inconceivable to you, which means you're always secondary, you're always enclosed within it.

627
00:37:32,097 --> 00:37:33,218
[SPEAKER_01]: What do I do next now?

628
00:37:36,802 --> 00:37:42,287
[SPEAKER_01]: What you do next is, whatever you're about to do, make sure it doesn't distract you from what you already know.

629
00:37:42,567 --> 00:37:43,528
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you do.

630
00:37:43,568 --> 00:37:44,729
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why sensory strength

631
00:37:45,843 --> 00:37:51,925
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically, although you don't know, sense restraint, restraining from centrality, restraining from acting out of anger and so on.

632
00:37:52,165 --> 00:37:55,886
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically emulating the right behavior.

633
00:37:57,646 --> 00:38:01,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, of course, just by emulating right behavior, you will not necessarily arrive at understanding.

634
00:38:01,947 --> 00:38:05,828
[SPEAKER_01]: But without having been emulating it rightly, you certainly have no ground for understanding.

635
00:38:06,929 --> 00:38:11,530
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's kind of learning how to act.

636
00:38:12,632 --> 00:38:25,416
[SPEAKER_01]: Learning how to basically re-tailor your life, so there are no underlying gratuitous assumptions of the external sense of control, mastery, ownership, who I don't partake in this.

637
00:38:25,476 --> 00:38:28,717
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, we all die, but basically you don't feel yourself, you die.

638
00:38:29,277 --> 00:38:40,501
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how did the Buddha sum up the re-tailoring of your life that would then remove these assumptions of external world outside of this coffin, and also remove any resistance to being in this coffin of this body, because

639
00:38:41,441 --> 00:38:46,804
[SPEAKER_01]: You choose to get upset on account of it, because you choose to not give up those gratuitous external assumptions.

640
00:38:47,464 --> 00:38:52,727
[SPEAKER_01]: How did the Buddha sum up that entire...

641
00:38:52,947 --> 00:38:56,068
[SPEAKER_00]: Various different ways, but... Noble Eightfold Path.

642
00:38:56,509 --> 00:39:01,291
[SPEAKER_01]: Noble Eightfold Path means there will be nothing left unaddressed.

643
00:39:01,551 --> 00:39:02,572
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, yeah.

644
00:39:05,193 --> 00:39:08,895
[SPEAKER_01]: But it all... it's all rooted in the same context, in that one understanding.

645
00:39:10,080 --> 00:39:11,300
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the right view comes first.

646
00:39:11,400 --> 00:39:13,321
[SPEAKER_01]: The right view determines the whole Noble Eightfold Path.

647
00:39:13,341 --> 00:39:17,282
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the right path.

648
00:39:17,562 --> 00:39:19,762
[SPEAKER_01]: Right view being view free from ātavāda.

649
00:39:20,202 --> 00:39:24,043
[SPEAKER_01]: Ātavāda being assuming the external sense of self in regard to the experience as a whole.

650
00:39:29,804 --> 00:39:31,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Something outside of this experience.

651
00:39:31,645 --> 00:39:32,645
[SPEAKER_00]: Something outside of the coffin.

652
00:39:34,215 --> 00:39:37,757
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, you don't need to say, okay, oh, that's a wrong thought.

653
00:39:37,877 --> 00:39:39,258
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no such thing as a wrong thought.

654
00:39:39,278 --> 00:39:41,339
[SPEAKER_01]: There's only wrong action on account of thought.

655
00:39:42,359 --> 00:39:45,761
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the thought that, oh, there's the external, okay, allow it to endure.

656
00:39:47,462 --> 00:39:49,703
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the external to this experience as a whole.

657
00:39:50,764 --> 00:39:52,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I even designate that for myself?

658
00:39:53,145 --> 00:39:53,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I think about it?

659
00:39:53,965 --> 00:39:58,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I have the vaguest notion of that external dimension in regard to my experience as a whole?

660
00:40:00,261 --> 00:40:03,162
[SPEAKER_01]: If this experience as a whole is not there first.

661
00:40:03,382 --> 00:40:05,823
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

662
00:40:05,843 --> 00:40:12,346
[SPEAKER_01]: So, any notion of external, that I have now, and I'm allowing to do, is always within.

663
00:40:13,667 --> 00:40:16,308
[SPEAKER_01]: This experience as a whole is always step beneath.

664
00:40:16,548 --> 00:40:16,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

665
00:40:17,248 --> 00:40:18,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter how far external I go.

666
00:40:18,789 --> 00:40:22,410
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what... doesn't matter how far you travel in this universe, you'll never reach the end.

667
00:40:22,870 --> 00:40:27,192
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always going to be step ahead of you, or step beneath you, whichever direction you want.

668
00:40:27,612 --> 00:40:28,973
[SPEAKER_01]: You want the end of the universe,

669
00:40:30,480 --> 00:40:31,560
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't reach it by traveling.

670
00:40:31,580 --> 00:40:35,441
[SPEAKER_01]: You reach it by giving up that necessary basis for any direction you can take.

671
00:40:36,222 --> 00:40:38,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Necessary basis of attachment to the body.

672
00:40:38,702 --> 00:40:39,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Ownership of the body.

673
00:40:40,343 --> 00:40:41,903
[SPEAKER_01]: Not seeing the anicca of the body.

674
00:40:42,823 --> 00:40:44,143
[SPEAKER_01]: Not seeing the body as saṅkhara.

675
00:40:45,084 --> 00:40:46,064
[SPEAKER_01]: Not understanding saṅkhara.

676
00:40:46,084 --> 00:40:47,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Not seeing it as uncontrollable.

677
00:40:47,384 --> 00:40:48,365
[SPEAKER_01]: Not seeing it as inaccessible.

678
00:40:48,385 --> 00:40:49,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Not seeing it as amicable.

679
00:40:49,445 --> 00:40:49,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

680
00:40:52,265 --> 00:40:54,306
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you want to stop assuming the external,

681
00:40:55,836 --> 00:41:00,199
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to start seeing it against the peripheral content, context of internal.

682
00:41:04,362 --> 00:41:07,644
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by denying, you know, I shouldn't be thinking this.

683
00:41:07,664 --> 00:41:08,364
[SPEAKER_01]: You are it.

684
00:41:08,585 --> 00:41:09,585
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what attavāra is.

685
00:41:09,605 --> 00:41:10,406
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you are.

686
00:41:10,646 --> 00:41:12,407
[SPEAKER_00]: By internal, you mean, like, body?

687
00:41:12,767 --> 00:41:13,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

688
00:41:13,428 --> 00:41:14,288
[SPEAKER_01]: By that container.

689
00:41:14,308 --> 00:41:17,150
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

690
00:41:17,170 --> 00:41:17,631
[SPEAKER_01]: So that...

691
00:41:18,231 --> 00:41:21,353
[SPEAKER_01]: So the notion of external, you want to recognize.

692
00:41:22,134 --> 00:41:23,274
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't try to get rid of it.

693
00:41:23,575 --> 00:41:25,116
[SPEAKER_01]: And certainly don't act out of it.

694
00:41:25,296 --> 00:41:25,496
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

695
00:41:26,068 --> 00:41:31,392
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will be acting out of it whenever you act out of desire, ill-will or distraction.

696
00:41:31,492 --> 00:41:32,053
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

697
00:41:32,513 --> 00:41:37,678
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why those things are wrong, because they perpetuate the gratuitous parasitic wrong assumption.

698
00:41:37,858 --> 00:41:38,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

699
00:41:38,778 --> 00:41:39,179
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

700
00:41:40,280 --> 00:41:43,202
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't act out of it, but you don't try to deny it either.

701
00:41:43,222 --> 00:41:44,063
[SPEAKER_01]: You allow it to endure.

702
00:41:44,083 --> 00:41:47,826
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is a genuine sense of, I'm external to this.

703
00:41:48,026 --> 00:41:49,447
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm independent of this.

704
00:41:49,547 --> 00:41:51,269
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then take the sense of independence.

705
00:41:51,429 --> 00:41:51,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

706
00:41:52,093 --> 00:41:53,894
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm independent to things that happen to me.

707
00:41:53,914 --> 00:41:54,415
[SPEAKER_01]: I can choose.

708
00:41:54,455 --> 00:41:55,075
[SPEAKER_01]: I can move left.

709
00:41:55,135 --> 00:41:55,716
[SPEAKER_01]: I can move right.

710
00:41:55,756 --> 00:41:56,356
[SPEAKER_01]: I can do this.

711
00:41:56,896 --> 00:42:00,679
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how would that sense of independence then be described?

712
00:42:00,779 --> 00:42:02,360
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's there, so you're not denying it.

713
00:42:02,961 --> 00:42:08,485
[SPEAKER_00]: It has something that has arisen thanks to the fact that there is this body here in the first place.

714
00:42:10,006 --> 00:42:10,406
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

715
00:42:10,486 --> 00:42:12,928
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can you then have any sense of independence

716
00:42:14,156 --> 00:42:14,937
[SPEAKER_01]: Without this body.

717
00:42:15,357 --> 00:42:15,517
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

718
00:42:16,218 --> 00:42:21,842
[SPEAKER_01]: So, is it then okay to look for the experience as a whole, anywhere else, except on the basis of this body?

719
00:42:21,882 --> 00:42:22,043
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

720
00:42:22,643 --> 00:42:26,666
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you conceive any experience except the experience on the basis of the body?

721
00:42:26,686 --> 00:42:27,587
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I think...

722
00:42:29,806 --> 00:42:40,954
[SPEAKER_00]: Personally, this notion of experience as a whole, for me, body, feeling, they kind of seem to... That's why I keep sort of going back to the mood, as in the general, the container, basically, the weather of mood.

723
00:42:40,974 --> 00:42:43,115
[SPEAKER_00]: If you've got a sense of the mood, there's nothing, there can't be anything...

724
00:42:43,255 --> 00:42:44,556
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no time to show up, perhaps.

725
00:42:44,656 --> 00:42:44,836
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

726
00:42:44,916 --> 00:42:45,637
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no time.

727
00:42:45,837 --> 00:42:46,718
[SPEAKER_01]: You are in that mood.

728
00:42:46,818 --> 00:42:47,018
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

729
00:42:47,558 --> 00:42:48,659
[SPEAKER_00]: And everything is revealed.

730
00:42:48,719 --> 00:42:55,501
[SPEAKER_01]: You can hope when the mood changes, it's going to be like this, it's going to be like that, but all of that is within... That hope is based on that mood.

731
00:42:55,902 --> 00:42:57,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the slightest hope.

732
00:42:57,562 --> 00:43:03,085
[SPEAKER_01]: The slightest conceiving is then seen fully against within this context it's contained in.

733
00:43:03,105 --> 00:43:03,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

734
00:43:04,245 --> 00:43:05,986
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you stop misconceiving traps.

735
00:43:07,140 --> 00:43:07,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

736
00:43:07,880 --> 00:43:08,460
[SPEAKER_00]: You always were.

737
00:43:08,480 --> 00:43:09,221
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the thing.

738
00:43:09,301 --> 00:43:11,621
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, but... Well, no, you're not trapped.

739
00:43:11,641 --> 00:43:12,242
[SPEAKER_00]: No, exactly.

740
00:43:12,262 --> 00:43:15,103
[SPEAKER_00]: So, trapped always implies, I want to get out.

741
00:43:15,843 --> 00:43:18,044
[SPEAKER_00]: And always implies, like, yeah, you were out before.

742
00:43:18,084 --> 00:43:19,064
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

743
00:43:20,464 --> 00:43:23,045
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just simply... No, you cannot... That's the nature of these things.

744
00:43:23,225 --> 00:43:24,506
[SPEAKER_01]: You're living within the trap.

745
00:43:24,786 --> 00:43:25,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

746
00:43:26,026 --> 00:43:28,227
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you don't necessarily need to be trapped.

747
00:43:28,467 --> 00:43:28,687
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

748
00:43:29,487 --> 00:43:31,888
[SPEAKER_01]: You get trapped when you choose to ignore.

749
00:43:33,432 --> 00:43:37,114
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you are trapped, by the trap you have to live in.

750
00:43:37,154 --> 00:43:41,235
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the simile of the herd, the sutta, the bait.

751
00:43:42,196 --> 00:43:49,159
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, is it like, could you say also, just not fighting the kind of nature thing?

752
00:43:49,239 --> 00:43:52,921
[SPEAKER_01]: In a way, yes, but see, people can do that for the pretext of fighting.

753
00:43:53,781 --> 00:43:55,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, just accept everything, just be here now.

754
00:43:56,062 --> 00:43:56,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

755
00:43:56,682 --> 00:43:57,983
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they want to get rid of it.

756
00:44:00,621 --> 00:44:04,623
[SPEAKER_01]: It's important where it's rooted, not like, okay, it's important what you do, of course.

757
00:44:05,303 --> 00:44:13,268
[SPEAKER_01]: But what you do, what you're doing, whether that will be helpful or unhelpful, skillful or unskillful, is whether it's rooted in you acting out of craving or not.

758
00:44:13,908 --> 00:44:16,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you're trying to get rid of things that displease you or not.

759
00:44:17,991 --> 00:44:28,857
[SPEAKER_01]: So, truly, you can say you will stop being trapped, you will stop being bothered by living in the trap, you will not... you will be unable to be careless in regard to trap, if you remove craving in regard to presently enduring mood.

760
00:44:29,676 --> 00:44:48,444
[SPEAKER_01]: Trying to get... trying to change the unpleasant mood, trying to change pleasant mood for more pleasant mood, trying to change unpleasant mood for less unpleasant mood, and trying to change the neutral mood for anything to feel just not... not nothing, yeah, because that's too frightening, just... just give me some form of distraction or excitement, whatever, whatever.

761
00:44:49,425 --> 00:44:56,908
[SPEAKER_00]: So, so, yeah, so this... so... so craving, you know, she says, is the origin of suffering.

762
00:44:57,848 --> 00:44:58,909
[SPEAKER_00]: And the most kind of direct.

763
00:45:00,381 --> 00:45:13,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Description is basically this sort of underlying... like wanting these things, which are the nature to change, to change.

764
00:45:14,826 --> 00:45:16,246
[SPEAKER_00]: Say it again, from the start.

765
00:45:18,287 --> 00:45:25,669
[SPEAKER_00]: So, craving is wanting these things, which are the nature to change, to change.

766
00:45:26,436 --> 00:45:27,657
[SPEAKER_00]: You want these things to change.

767
00:45:28,358 --> 00:45:34,863
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you've already been affected by change.

768
00:45:35,583 --> 00:45:39,466
[SPEAKER_00]: You've been bothered by their nature, which has nothing to do with you.

769
00:45:40,147 --> 00:45:41,408
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why you accept their nature.

770
00:45:41,768 --> 00:45:41,988
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

771
00:45:42,949 --> 00:45:45,791
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, by accepting their nature, which is to change,

772
00:45:46,612 --> 00:45:48,374
[SPEAKER_00]: You stop wanting that to change.

773
00:45:48,694 --> 00:45:48,894
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

774
00:45:48,914 --> 00:45:50,075
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that doesn't make any sense.

775
00:45:50,095 --> 00:45:50,376
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

776
00:45:50,996 --> 00:45:54,359
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's pretty much the the root of every motivation that an ignorant mind would do.

777
00:45:54,880 --> 00:45:55,961
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is wanting something to change.

778
00:45:56,041 --> 00:45:56,221
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

779
00:45:56,401 --> 00:45:57,382
[SPEAKER_00]: That your action is.

780
00:45:57,583 --> 00:45:58,764
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, that is action, isn't it?

781
00:45:58,864 --> 00:46:00,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Action is wanting things to change.

782
00:46:00,746 --> 00:46:01,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

783
00:46:01,406 --> 00:46:01,646
[SPEAKER_00]: Always.

784
00:46:02,047 --> 00:46:02,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

785
00:46:02,687 --> 00:46:03,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Always.

786
00:46:03,688 --> 00:46:04,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Good action or bad action.

787
00:46:04,849 --> 00:46:05,050
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

788
00:46:06,211 --> 00:46:07,292
[SPEAKER_01]: And why do you want things to change?

789
00:46:07,412 --> 00:46:08,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they've changed.

790
00:46:08,333 --> 00:46:09,274
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they've changed.

791
00:46:09,374 --> 00:46:11,256
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they've changed in a way that I don't like.

792
00:46:12,434 --> 00:46:13,054
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is always.

793
00:46:13,354 --> 00:46:13,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

794
00:46:14,055 --> 00:46:16,035
[SPEAKER_01]: See, when they change for better, you want more.

795
00:46:16,155 --> 00:46:16,976
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's right.

796
00:46:17,136 --> 00:46:17,316
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

797
00:46:17,976 --> 00:46:19,657
[SPEAKER_01]: When they change for worse, you want less.

798
00:46:20,077 --> 00:46:22,658
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, you want on account of change.

799
00:46:22,678 --> 00:46:23,058
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

800
00:46:23,158 --> 00:46:25,399
[SPEAKER_01]: And what is it... what is it that you could possibly want?

801
00:46:25,879 --> 00:46:26,699
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, that's another change.

802
00:46:26,719 --> 00:46:26,899
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

803
00:46:27,760 --> 00:46:28,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, so what you're doing...

804
00:46:28,360 --> 00:46:34,542
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because of the change I suffer, and in return to free myself from suffering, I run back straight into the change.

805
00:46:34,562 --> 00:46:34,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

806
00:46:34,982 --> 00:46:36,403
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a definition of abate.

807
00:46:38,638 --> 00:46:49,342
[SPEAKER_01]: You get hooked, and because you're hooked, you get hooked even harder, because you're just fully on, full on, like resisting it, rejecting it, and each time you do that, the hook gets deeper and deeper.

808
00:46:54,523 --> 00:47:05,907
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way to release yourself from the hook is to stop resisting the things on that peripheral level, on the level of saṅkhara, on the level of determinations,

809
00:47:08,965 --> 00:47:11,627
[SPEAKER_01]: On that level of giving, where you recognize that you have no control.

810
00:47:12,428 --> 00:47:16,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Or recognizing that this... Or on the level of that nature, as the Buddha would say.

811
00:47:17,472 --> 00:47:33,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Or can we say... no matter how much I resist this now, which I'm doing, that is fully enclosed within this.

812
00:47:34,225 --> 00:47:34,786
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

813
00:47:35,066 --> 00:47:37,408
[SPEAKER_01]: By developing this passion in regard to your own craving.

814
00:47:39,104 --> 00:47:39,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

815
00:47:39,704 --> 00:47:41,005
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you can do it like that as well.

816
00:47:42,245 --> 00:47:46,526
[SPEAKER_01]: Realizing that it doesn't matter if I go, if I don't, if I say yes, if I say no.

817
00:47:46,706 --> 00:47:47,666
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't make any difference.

818
00:47:47,846 --> 00:47:48,867
[SPEAKER_01]: Cannot step outside of it.

819
00:47:49,547 --> 00:47:50,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you regain the perspective.

820
00:47:51,107 --> 00:47:51,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

821
00:47:53,628 --> 00:47:54,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Outside of what?

822
00:47:54,928 --> 00:47:57,449
[SPEAKER_01]: Outside of this thing that I'm attending right here, right now.

823
00:47:57,489 --> 00:47:57,669
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

824
00:47:58,389 --> 00:48:07,312
[SPEAKER_01]: I stop assuming the genuine, stop conceiving the genuine existence of the outside of this which can only be known on the basis of this inside I'm enclosed with.

825
00:48:07,372 --> 00:48:07,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

826
00:48:09,479 --> 00:48:14,402
[SPEAKER_01]: On a most practical level, you cannot experience anything outside of your body.

827
00:48:14,502 --> 00:48:15,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Changing the order of things.

828
00:48:15,863 --> 00:48:18,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Engaging with things on account of, like, whatever your desires.

829
00:48:20,105 --> 00:48:21,506
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your body that engages with it.

830
00:48:22,406 --> 00:48:26,749
[SPEAKER_01]: And your thought of the world outside of your body is a thought that's within this body.

831
00:48:27,850 --> 00:48:29,030
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody says it's a wrong thought.

832
00:48:29,310 --> 00:48:31,692
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's a wrong thought if it misconceives the right order.

833
00:48:32,845 --> 00:48:37,629
[SPEAKER_01]: But otherwise, it's just a thought of outside, which I now know is fully within this body.

834
00:48:38,190 --> 00:48:42,274
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you are gripping something with your hand, what is your experience there?

835
00:48:42,654 --> 00:48:45,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you actually accessing the matter of this thing you're touching?

836
00:48:46,017 --> 00:48:49,200
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you just have the experience of your body touching something?

837
00:48:50,061 --> 00:48:54,025
[SPEAKER_01]: See, even notion of something applies a certain external world.

838
00:48:54,045 --> 00:48:57,128
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you even know that without this basis of the body?

839
00:48:58,647 --> 00:49:00,348
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that doesn't mean everything is in my mind.

840
00:49:00,368 --> 00:49:03,149
[SPEAKER_01]: It means everything is within the relationship of body and the mind.

841
00:49:03,269 --> 00:49:16,014
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, it's funny, like, even, like, in the suttas, it says, like, you know, the eye and sights, and the ear and sounds, and I guess, that kind of... you can... okay, so, there's the body over here, and then there's those things that are separate.

842
00:49:16,034 --> 00:49:16,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

843
00:49:16,914 --> 00:49:18,935
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, the suttas don't actually say that.

844
00:49:19,135 --> 00:49:19,315
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

845
00:49:19,815 --> 00:49:24,017
[SPEAKER_01]: But what, like, because of people's views, they end up having that conclusion.

846
00:49:24,697 --> 00:49:25,417
[SPEAKER_01]: The suttas say

847
00:49:28,275 --> 00:49:30,156
[SPEAKER_01]: And then consciousness comes together.

848
00:49:30,196 --> 00:49:30,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

849
00:49:30,937 --> 00:49:32,658
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eyes and sights are on one level.

850
00:49:32,878 --> 00:49:33,078
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

851
00:49:33,318 --> 00:49:34,339
[SPEAKER_01]: Share the same level.

852
00:49:34,379 --> 00:49:34,539
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

853
00:49:34,859 --> 00:49:37,421
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not eyes in here, sights on the other level out there.

854
00:49:37,461 --> 00:49:37,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

855
00:49:38,301 --> 00:49:40,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Eyes and sights and corresponding consciousness comes.

856
00:49:40,943 --> 00:49:42,764
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why one is pressured by sights.

857
00:49:43,725 --> 00:49:46,566
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sights are on the level of your eyes.

858
00:49:47,127 --> 00:49:48,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Eyes are on the level of the sights.

859
00:49:50,129 --> 00:49:53,291
[SPEAKER_01]: And usually, because of the wrong view that people assume eyes are in me,

860
00:49:54,262 --> 00:49:55,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Sights are outside.

861
00:49:55,604 --> 00:49:56,945
[SPEAKER_01]: Another assumption of outside.

862
00:49:58,547 --> 00:49:59,428
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to reverse it.

863
00:50:00,189 --> 00:50:03,513
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you find that sights are within the eyes that are in you.

864
00:50:04,253 --> 00:50:05,155
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you know sights?

865
00:50:05,475 --> 00:50:06,456
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you imagine sights?

866
00:50:06,476 --> 00:50:09,039
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you even fantasize about sights?

867
00:50:09,880 --> 00:50:10,981
[SPEAKER_01]: If you've never had eyes.

868
00:50:13,834 --> 00:50:14,714
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable.

869
00:50:14,875 --> 00:50:15,795
[SPEAKER_00]: Utterly inconceivable.

870
00:50:16,195 --> 00:50:20,397
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, here you are, thinking you can access all these sites independent of your eyes.

871
00:50:20,497 --> 00:50:26,560
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any thing that you can imagine, visible, requires the necessary basis of the body.

872
00:50:26,840 --> 00:50:27,861
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the Buddha said.

873
00:50:27,881 --> 00:50:31,963
[SPEAKER_01]: The entire universe, literally, you travel to different cosmos.

874
00:50:32,543 --> 00:50:33,924
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still within the body.

875
00:50:33,984 --> 00:50:36,205
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot step outside of this body.

876
00:50:36,225 --> 00:50:38,006
[SPEAKER_00]: And it can only be in those six domains.

877
00:50:38,921 --> 00:50:39,701
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, of course, yeah.

878
00:50:40,041 --> 00:50:43,523
[SPEAKER_00]: That's if you want to look at the aspects of that one unit of the body.

879
00:50:44,103 --> 00:50:47,304
[SPEAKER_01]: It's one unit, one base of the six senses.

880
00:50:52,566 --> 00:50:57,968
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, the thought of outside is now seen against the backdrop of fully enclosed within this body.

881
00:51:06,619 --> 00:51:15,948
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you start seeing it like that, there will be no more basis for the notion of, I am outside, independent of this experience as a whole.

882
00:51:15,968 --> 00:51:18,591
[SPEAKER_01]: It means there will be no more out of other.

883
00:51:22,134 --> 00:51:25,238
[SPEAKER_00]: And, yeah, I was just thinking about that.

884
00:51:31,821 --> 00:51:42,904
[SPEAKER_00]: You're subjected to that which is abrupt, uncontrollable.

885
00:51:43,224 --> 00:51:46,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can't repossess it from its roots.

886
00:51:46,425 --> 00:51:49,466
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't tell it to be like this, or not age, or not die.

887
00:51:49,866 --> 00:51:50,326
[SPEAKER_01]: You just can't.

888
00:51:50,406 --> 00:51:50,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable.

889
00:51:52,344 --> 00:51:57,646
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just don't forget that as the ultimate context whenever you're about to act, speak or think.

890
00:51:58,106 --> 00:52:01,187
[SPEAKER_00]: And whenever you're acting, speaking and thinking, it's not like that.

891
00:52:02,388 --> 00:52:03,268
[SPEAKER_00]: You're denying that.

892
00:52:03,408 --> 00:52:05,889
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just including that within that broader context.

893
00:52:06,069 --> 00:52:06,369
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

894
00:52:06,550 --> 00:52:06,970
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

895
00:52:07,310 --> 00:52:11,892
[SPEAKER_00]: You think against this backdrop, means the context remains despite of your actions and your thoughts.

896
00:52:11,912 --> 00:52:11,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

897
00:52:12,272 --> 00:52:15,153
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as you keep saying, it's about establishing a context.

898
00:52:15,725 --> 00:52:15,965
[SPEAKER_01]: Always.

899
00:52:16,246 --> 00:52:16,526
[SPEAKER_01]: Always.

900
00:52:16,947 --> 00:52:17,868
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the right view is.

901
00:52:18,128 --> 00:52:18,769
[SPEAKER_00]: The right context.

902
00:52:18,789 --> 00:52:19,490
[SPEAKER_00]: The right context.

903
00:52:19,830 --> 00:52:20,011
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

904
00:52:20,451 --> 00:52:24,977
[SPEAKER_00]: And you... until... until that context is unshakable.

905
00:52:25,117 --> 00:52:26,839
[SPEAKER_00]: Because nothing can't not be included in it.

906
00:52:27,440 --> 00:52:28,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

907
00:52:28,241 --> 00:52:32,607
[SPEAKER_01]: And that context is unshakable is when you yourself stop shaking it through your carelessness.

908
00:52:33,828 --> 00:52:34,989
[SPEAKER_01]: He was always unshakable.

909
00:52:35,069 --> 00:52:35,690
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Buddha said.

910
00:52:36,090 --> 00:52:38,272
[SPEAKER_01]: Our consciousness was always pure.

911
00:52:38,392 --> 00:52:39,993
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't really corrupt things.

912
00:52:40,154 --> 00:52:44,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Only ignoring that, not knowing that through your ignorance, that's what corruption is there.

913
00:52:59,878 --> 00:53:06,664
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether it's the mood, whether it's the body, literally, it's a presently enduring context in regard to anything, including right this, right now.

914
00:53:07,505 --> 00:53:09,867
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, so, like, next time I think about the outside...

915
00:53:10,127 --> 00:53:12,309
[SPEAKER_01]: These thoughts are within the presently enduring mood.

916
00:53:14,111 --> 00:53:15,953
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you imagine outside of that mood?

917
00:53:16,293 --> 00:53:17,574
[SPEAKER_01]: I can imagine before or after.

918
00:53:17,995 --> 00:53:19,316
[SPEAKER_01]: I can imagine moving within it.

919
00:53:19,876 --> 00:53:21,318
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you imagine outside of it?

920
00:53:22,271 --> 00:53:24,732
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you imagine me being first, Maud being second?

921
00:53:24,892 --> 00:53:25,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable!

922
00:53:26,533 --> 00:53:27,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Think about it.

923
00:53:27,213 --> 00:53:33,156
[SPEAKER_01]: The more you think about it, the clearer it will become, the more significant that context will be.

924
00:53:33,876 --> 00:53:36,198
[SPEAKER_01]: The more easily accessible that context will be.

925
00:53:37,398 --> 00:53:42,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means that the better mindfulness of it will be established.

926
00:53:43,801 --> 00:53:45,722
[SPEAKER_01]: To the mind, as we were saying in that Satipaṭṭhāna,

927
00:53:46,310 --> 00:53:48,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Or he develops mindfulness to the extent necessary.

928
00:53:48,591 --> 00:53:52,232
[SPEAKER_01]: Or he develops context to the extent necessary for final knowledge.

929
00:53:52,272 --> 00:53:56,593
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, for the knowledge that cannot pervert the order with gratuitous assumption anymore.

930
00:53:57,153 --> 00:53:59,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Take second to be the first and first to be the second.

931
00:54:00,114 --> 00:54:02,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Take outside to be the inside and inside to be outside.

932
00:54:02,654 --> 00:54:03,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable.

933
00:54:03,675 --> 00:54:08,776
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you develop the context that been including everything sufficient amount of time.

934
00:54:09,196 --> 00:54:11,417
[SPEAKER_01]: Any thought, any movement, what if, any doubt.

935
00:54:11,677 --> 00:54:14,378
[SPEAKER_01]: That's again, free from attavāda means free from doubt.

936
00:54:15,038 --> 00:54:15,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Because

937
00:54:15,938 --> 00:54:20,301
[SPEAKER_01]: You can doubt the content, you can't doubt the present nature of enduring doubt.

938
00:54:21,462 --> 00:54:23,964
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the content of the doubt...

939
00:54:25,325 --> 00:54:27,907
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because there is a doubt enduring there.

940
00:54:29,088 --> 00:54:30,669
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that doubt cannot take you outside.

941
00:54:30,689 --> 00:54:32,030
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter how much you think.

942
00:54:32,530 --> 00:54:33,070
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

943
00:54:33,451 --> 00:54:36,273
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead of focusing on clarifying the content of your doubt,

944
00:54:36,909 --> 00:54:41,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Just focus on including its nature within the necessary basis of the five aggregates.

945
00:54:41,233 --> 00:54:43,115
[SPEAKER_01]: Within the necessary basis of presently enduring mood.

946
00:54:43,495 --> 00:54:46,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Within the necessary basis of presently given body there.

947
00:54:46,358 --> 00:54:49,341
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not ignoring the context that that has manifest.

948
00:54:49,521 --> 00:54:49,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

949
00:54:50,002 --> 00:54:56,108
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you're perfectly capable of enduring doubt and not suffering on account of it and certainly not falling back to the wrong view.

950
00:54:56,668 --> 00:54:58,290
[SPEAKER_01]: Wrong view means wrong assumption.

951
00:54:58,330 --> 00:54:59,891
[SPEAKER_01]: Wrong assumption means perverted order.

